// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: meta-parameters, refinement, declared-but-unenforced
// @expected-error: E500
// @description: PINS A GAP — a refinement on a META PARAMETER is
//   accepted and constrains nothing.
//
// The grammar admits it:
//
//     meta_param = identifier , ':' , 'meta' , type_expr , [ refinement ] ;
//
// so a width can be declared to be one of a fixed set, at compile time,
// and the compiler will say nothing when it is not. This spec expects
// the refusal that does not happen yet; it is red on purpose and turns
// green when the position is wired to the refinement emitter.
//
// The control below is what makes this one narrow question rather than
// "meta parameters are unfinished": the width IS part of type identity,
// and passing a `Digest<8>` where a `Digest<4>` is required IS refused.
// So the parameter is real. Only its refinement is inert.
//
// This is the worst class of defect in this codebase's taxonomy — a
// feature that is present, accepted, documented in the grammar, and
// does nothing, so everything built on it inherits a guarantee that was
// never made. A digest width is exactly the thing someone would lean
// on: mixing a 32-byte and a 64-byte digest is a real supply-chain bug
// class, and this reads like the language rules it out.

public type Digest<N: meta USize {it == 4 || it == 8}> is {
    bytes: [Byte; N],
};

fn main() {
    // 5 is not admitted by the predicate. This must be refused.
    let bad: Digest<5> = Digest { bytes: [1_u8, 2_u8, 3_u8, 4_u8, 5_u8] };
    print(f"len={bad.bytes.len()}");
}
